## qspec suite cache pre-warm (INC.2).
##
## NOT a spec — the runner's glob is `*_spec.qz`, so this file is never
## executed as a test. `run_qspec` compiles it ONCE, single-threaded,
## with a writable `.qzmod` cache before the worker pool starts. Every
## module imported here (and its transitive imports) gets a complete
## `.qzmod` artifact — the encoder ships all of a module's declarations,
## not a reachable subset, so a single compile fully warms each module.
## The workers then compile each spec `--cache-ro` against this cache:
## pure reads, so no two workers ever race on a write.
##
## Coverage = the qspec framework, which every one of the 809 specs
## imports, plus everything the framework transitively pulls in (the
## formatter, matchers, Progress/Live UI, subprocess driver, …). That
## is the dominant shared compile cost.
##
## NOT a kitchen sink: importing every stdlib module here fails — they
## collide on effect declarations (QZ0612 duplicate `FileIo`) and on
## effect-context requirements (QZ0611). A spec importing a stdlib
## module beyond the framework still compiles correctly under
## `--cache-ro` (cache miss → cold compile, no write); it just misses
## the warm-compile speedup for that one module.

import * from qspec
import * from qspec/subprocess
import * from qspec/property

def main(): Int
  return 0
end
